HI! And welcome to the real world of Linux!  :)
With Dec (er.. excuse me Compaq) dumping NT support, I think we're going to see
a lot of you types here! Glad to see this. This may be the single biggest boon
to Linux ever contrived!

On to those of your questions that I can answer:

Jonas Gustavsson wrote:
> 
> 
> What windowmanager is generaly considered to be the most stable/supported one out there(MetroLink, Xi or Xfree86)? Preferably hardware accelerated stuff.

MetroX, X1, XFree86, are not window managers. They're X graphics systems.
(they're the "back end" to the window managers). 
	Which to use"
	They're all good. However:
	XFree86 is free. It's a solid performer, and so widely used that it probably
has the best support structure that you could as for. But be aware: It's a free
product. It's not without it's problems. Configuring it is a little harder than
some of the other X systems. I use it on my Alpha, and it's never let me down.

	MetroX (By Metrolink). Again, a solid performer, but limited in it's support of
graphics cards & monitors. XFree86 supports a wider base of cards. You have to
pay for MetroX, but the price is reasonable (_about_ $40 USD or so). It's easier
to configure, if that's an issue. And the support from Metrolink is fair. I'm
running it on an Intel box. Again, a solid performer. But be aware: You still
need to install "parts" of XFree86. There are subsystems built into Linux that
require it. Not a big deal.

	Xi. The Rolls Royce of them all. It's the most expensive out there (That I'm
aware of). I've never run it, but know folks that do. performance wise: It's
probably the fastest of the bunch, but can be problematic. 

Personally: I'd start with XFree86. It's included with Redhat. If it doesn't
work for you, then look into one of the alternatives.

> 
> The same goes for Audio(OSS or something else)?
>
OSS. Absolutely nothing else will do.  :)
	Actually, there is good sound support built into the kernel. I'd look into that
first. If you can't get it to run your sound card, then look into OSS.
 
> is there any DDR(PVR, Digisuite, ...) support or any support in progress?

	Pass...  Dunno!
> 
> any Faq´s around of Linux/NT networking stuff(samba)?
> 
When you install SAMBA, there is a doc directory
(/usr/docs/samba-<version.number>. It's chocked full of information,
documentation, examples, and sample smb.conf files for various configurations.
In short: Most of the help you'll need.
However, should you still need help getting it running (and it "can" be
challenging.. I know, I was building one last night), you have several
resources:
Check out:	http://samba.org	(the samba home site)
or you can post questions here!  :)


> anyone know when the API sponsored development package will be released(cygnus I think)?
	Pass . . . Dunno

> 
> any ETA on the DEC GEM C/C++ compiler Beta??

	Ditto . . . Dunno
> 

> and finaly and the most inportant: ANY GAMES??(realy need friday night Quake fests at the office)

Yeah. Quake is out there for Alpha. Try looking at quakeworld for a starter.
They should have something, or know where to get it. I think I saw both client &
server side pkgs there not too long ago. Been a while though. I don't so many
games.

Hope that helps!

	Ric


